hjs-008.text:
I will build a tagged template function that walks through the template literal parts and interpolations, building a query string where each simple value becomes a numbered placeholder (`$1`, `$2`, …) and collecting the values in order. When an interpolation is itself a result of `sqlTag`, I will inline its `text` and adjust its placeholders by adding the current offset, merging its `values` into the main array. If an interpolation is an array, I will treat it as a parenthesised list of placeholders, again renumbering any nested placeholders. Repeated values are naturally handled because each interpolation creates a new placeholder.

```javascript
function sqlTag(strings, ...values) {
  const result = { text: '', values: [] };
  let offset = 0;

  strings.forEach((str, i) => {
    result.text += str;
    if (i < values.length) {
      const val = values[i];
      if (val && typeof val === 'object' && val.text && Array.isArray(val.values)) {
        // Inline another sqlTag result
        const subOffset = offset + result.values.length;
        const subPlaceholders = val.text.replace(/\$(\d+)/g, (_, num) => `\$${parseInt(num, 10) + subOffset}`);
        result.text += subPlaceholders;
        result.values.push(...val.values);
        offset += val.values.length;
      } else if (Array.isArray(val)) {
        // Treat as a list of placeholders
        const placeholders = val.map((_, idx) => `$${offset + idx + 1}`).join(', ');
        result.text += `(${placeholders})`;
        result.values.push(...val);
        offset += val.length;
      } else {
        // Simple value placeholder
        const placeholder = `$${offset + 1}`;
        result.text += placeholder;
        result.values.push(val);
        offset += 1;
      }
    }
  });

  return result;
}

module.exports = { sqlTag };
```

